1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,470
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science A,

2
00:00:05,470 --> 00:00:11,083
Unit 1, part three: topics one point
eleven to one point fifteen. We'll use the

3
00:00:11,083 --> 00:00:14,635
Math class, create objects, and work with
Strings.

4
00:00:15,808 --> 00:00:19,968
Java's Math class is a toolbox of ready
made calculations.

5
00:00:20,418 --> 00:00:25,219
All its methods are static, so you call
them with the class name: Math dot, then

6
00:00:25,219 --> 00:00:25,879
the method.

7
00:00:26,329 --> 00:00:28,911
Here are the ones you need for the AP
exam.

8
00:00:30,061 --> 00:00:36,013
Math dot abs gives the absolute value.
Math dot abs of negative seven is seven.

9
00:00:36,463 --> 00:00:42,574
Math dot pow raises a number to a power.
Math dot pow of two and three is eight

10
00:00:42,574 --> 00:00:46,596
point zero. Notice that pow always returns
a double.

11
00:00:47,046 --> 00:00:52,736
Math dot square root gives the square
root. Math dot square root of eighty one

12
00:00:52,736 --> 00:00:55,217
is nine point zero, also a double.

13
00:00:56,367 --> 00:01:02,630
Math dot random gives a random double from
zero point zero up to, but not including,

14
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:03,748
one point zero.

15
00:01:04,198 --> 00:01:09,998
Every call gives a different value, like
zero point three seven, or zero point nine

16
00:01:09,998 --> 00:01:10,278
one.

17
00:01:11,428 --> 00:01:14,884
To get a random int in a range, stretch,
shift, and cast.

18
00:01:15,334 --> 00:01:20,689
Multiply by how many values you want, cast
to int, then add the smallest value.

19
00:01:21,139 --> 00:01:27,218
For a die roll from one to six: Math dot
random times six gives zero up to six.

20
00:01:27,218 --> 00:01:31,528
Casting gives zero to five. Adding one
gives one to six.

21
00:01:32,678 --> 00:01:38,033
Here's the general pattern. For a random
int from min to max, including both ends:

22
00:01:38,483 --> 00:01:44,072
cast Math dot random, times max minus min
plus one, to an int, then add min.

23
00:01:44,522 --> 00:01:48,853
The plus one is easy to forget, and it's a
favorite AP exam trap.

24
00:01:50,003 --> 00:01:54,951
Your turn. Write an expression for a
random int from ten to twenty, including

25
00:01:54,951 --> 00:01:55,272
both.

26
00:01:55,722 --> 00:01:57,002
Pause the video now.

27
00:01:57,452 --> 00:02:03,169
There are eleven values, so it's int cast
of Math dot random times eleven, plus ten.

28
00:02:03,619 --> 00:02:05,753
Next, we'll start working with objects.

29
00:02:06,903 --> 00:02:12,358
Java is an object oriented language.
Almost everything you build is made of

30
00:02:12,358 --> 00:02:12,940
objects.

31
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:18,020
In this lesson, you'll learn what objects
are, and how they relate to classes.

32
00:02:19,170 --> 00:02:24,255
A class is a blueprint. It describes what
kind of data something has, and what it

33
00:02:24,255 --> 00:02:24,695
can do.

34
00:02:25,145 --> 00:02:30,729
An object is one specific thing built from
that blueprint. We say the object is an

35
00:02:30,729 --> 00:02:32,228
instance of the class.

36
00:02:32,678 --> 00:02:36,240
One class can be used to build as many
objects as you need.

37
00:02:37,390 --> 00:02:39,353
Let's imagine a class called Lamp.

38
00:02:39,803 --> 00:02:43,536
Every Lamp has attributes, like its color,
and whether it's on.

39
00:02:43,986 --> 00:02:48,850
And every Lamp has behaviors, which are
its methods, like turn on and turn off.

40
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,750
Now build three Lamp objects.

41
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:57,492
Each one has its own copy of the
attributes. One is red and on. One is blue

42
00:02:57,492 --> 00:02:59,538
and off. One is green and on.

43
00:02:59,988 --> 00:03:04,682
Turning off the red lamp changes only that
lamp. The others aren't affected.

44
00:03:05,832 --> 00:03:10,397
In Java, a variable of a class type
doesn't hold the object itself.

45
00:03:10,847 --> 00:03:15,412
It holds a reference, which tells Java
where the object lives in memory.

46
00:03:15,862 --> 00:03:20,431
That's why class types are called
reference types, unlike int and double,

47
00:03:20,431 --> 00:03:22,497
which hold their values directly.

48
00:03:23,647 --> 00:03:29,155
Your turn. String is a class, and the text
hello in quotes is a String. Which one is

49
00:03:29,155 --> 00:03:29,876
the object?

50
00:03:30,326 --> 00:03:31,606
Pause the video now.

51
00:03:32,056 --> 00:03:36,216
The text hello is the object. It's an
instance of the String class.

52
00:03:36,666 --> 00:03:39,312
Next, we'll create objects with the
keyword new.

53
00:03:40,461 --> 00:03:45,672
You know that objects are built from
classes. But how do you actually build one

54
00:03:45,672 --> 00:03:46,200
in Java?

55
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:51,127
In this lesson, you'll create objects with
the keyword new, and see where they're

56
00:03:51,127 --> 00:03:51,514
stored.

57
00:03:52,664 --> 00:03:57,762
To create an object, use the keyword new,
followed by a call to a constructor.

58
00:03:58,212 --> 00:04:01,306
Lamp desk equals new Lamp, with the
argument red.

59
00:04:01,756 --> 00:04:06,710
This builds a new Lamp object in memory,
and stores a reference to it in the

60
00:04:06,710 --> 00:04:07,622
variable desk.

61
00:04:08,772 --> 00:04:14,122
A constructor is a special method that
sets up a new object. It has the same name

62
00:04:14,122 --> 00:04:14,980
as the class.

63
00:04:15,430 --> 00:04:20,789
Constructors can be overloaded. Lamp might
have one constructor with no parameters,

64
00:04:20,789 --> 00:04:22,790
and another that takes a color.

65
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:27,230
Java picks the constructor whose
parameters match the arguments.

66
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:32,049
A reference variable can also hold the
special value null.

67
00:04:32,499 --> 00:04:36,190
null means the variable doesn't refer to
any object at all.

68
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:41,551
If you try to call a method on a null
reference, the program crashes with a Null

69
00:04:41,551 --> 00:04:42,656
Pointer Exception.

70
00:04:43,806 --> 00:04:46,366
Here's something that surprises many
students.

71
00:04:46,816 --> 00:04:52,549
If you write Lamp other equals desk, no
new lamp is made. Both variables now refer

72
00:04:52,549 --> 00:04:53,877
to the same object.

73
00:04:54,327 --> 00:04:59,426
So calling other dot turn off also turns
off desk. There's only one lamp.

74
00:05:00,576 --> 00:05:06,574
Your turn. How many Lamp objects are
created by these lines? Lamp a equals new

75
00:05:06,574 --> 00:05:10,112
Lamp. Lamp b equals a. Lamp c equals new
Lamp.

76
00:05:10,562 --> 00:05:11,842
Pause the video now.

77
00:05:12,292 --> 00:05:17,753
Two. Each new creates one object. Lamp b
equals a just copies the reference.

78
00:05:18,203 --> 00:05:21,168
Next, we'll call instance methods on our
objects.

79
00:05:22,319 --> 00:05:26,180
Objects can do things. Their behaviors are
instance methods.

80
00:05:26,630 --> 00:05:31,097
In this lesson, you'll call instance
methods, and see how they differ from

81
00:05:31,097 --> 00:05:31,942
class methods.

82
00:05:33,092 --> 00:05:38,101
To call an instance method, write the
object reference, a dot, then the method

83
00:05:38,101 --> 00:05:39,322
name and arguments.

84
00:05:39,772 --> 00:05:41,820
For example, desk dot turn off.

85
00:05:42,270 --> 00:05:47,927
The method works on that specific object's
data. Calling it on desk changes desk, not

86
00:05:47,927 --> 00:05:48,926
any other lamp.

87
00:05:50,076 --> 00:05:55,429
Compare this with a class method. Math dot
abs is called on the class, because it

88
00:05:55,429 --> 00:05:57,478
doesn't need any object's data.

89
00:05:57,928 --> 00:06:02,685
But the length of a String depends on
which String you ask. So length is an

90
00:06:02,685 --> 00:06:05,032
instance method, called on an object.

91
00:06:06,182 --> 00:06:08,550
Let's try it. String word equals hello.

92
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,118
word dot length returns five, because
hello has five characters.

93
00:06:13,568 --> 00:06:18,859
Like any non void method, the returned
value can be stored, printed, or used in

94
00:06:18,859 --> 00:06:19,797
an expression.

95
00:06:20,947 --> 00:06:25,847
Some instance methods are void. They
change the object, or do an action, and

96
00:06:25,847 --> 00:06:26,814
return nothing.

97
00:06:27,264 --> 00:06:32,776
Others return a value about the object.
Either way, you must call them on a real

98
00:06:32,776 --> 00:06:33,258
object.

99
00:06:33,708 --> 00:06:38,380
Calling an instance method on null crashes
with a Null Pointer Exception.

100
00:06:39,530 --> 00:06:45,909
Your turn. String w equals Java. What does
w dot length, plus one, evaluate to?

101
00:06:46,359 --> 00:06:47,639
Pause the video now.

102
00:06:48,089 --> 00:06:53,145
Java has four characters, so w dot length
is four, plus one is five.

103
00:06:53,595 --> 00:06:56,710
Next, we'll explore the most important
String methods.

104
00:06:57,859 --> 00:07:01,080
Strings are everywhere: names, messages,
passwords.

105
00:07:01,530 --> 00:07:07,118
The AP exam tests a handful of String
methods again and again. In this lesson,

106
00:07:07,118 --> 00:07:09,338
you'll learn every one of them.

107
00:07:10,488 --> 00:07:16,014
First, indexes. Each character in a String
has a position, and positions start at

108
00:07:16,014 --> 00:07:16,355
zero.

109
00:07:16,805 --> 00:07:23,154
In the word computer, c is at index zero,
o is at index one, and the last character,

110
00:07:23,154 --> 00:07:24,741
r, is at index seven.

111
00:07:25,191 --> 00:07:30,165
length returns eight, the number of
characters. So the last index is always

112
00:07:30,165 --> 00:07:31,292
length minus one.

113
00:07:32,442 --> 00:07:35,728
substring with two arguments returns part
of the String.

114
00:07:36,178 --> 00:07:40,508
It starts at the first index, and stops
just before the second index.

115
00:07:40,958 --> 00:07:47,504
So computer dot substring of three and six
is p u t, the characters at indexes three,

116
00:07:47,504 --> 00:07:48,660
four, and five.

117
00:07:49,810 --> 00:07:54,098
substring with one argument starts at that
index and goes to the end.

118
00:07:54,548 --> 00:07:57,150
computer dot substring of three is puter.

119
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:03,019
If an index is out of range, Java throws a
String Index Out Of Bounds Exception.

120
00:08:04,169 --> 00:08:08,692
index Of searches for text and returns the
index where it first appears.

121
00:08:09,142 --> 00:08:11,872
computer dot index Of put returns three.

122
00:08:12,322 --> 00:08:16,034
If the text isn't found, index Of returns
negative one.

123
00:08:17,184 --> 00:08:22,066
To compare Strings, use equals, not double
equals. Equals checks whether the

124
00:08:22,066 --> 00:08:23,158
characters match.

125
00:08:23,608 --> 00:08:28,736
compare To checks alphabetical order. It
returns a negative number if the first

126
00:08:28,736 --> 00:08:33,800
String comes earlier, zero if they're
equal, and a positive number if it comes

127
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:34,189
later.

128
00:08:34,639 --> 00:08:37,946
For example, apple compare To banana is
negative.

129
00:08:39,096 --> 00:08:44,301
Strings are immutable. No String method
ever changes the original String.

130
00:08:44,751 --> 00:08:49,934
Methods like substring return a new
String. To keep the result, assign it to a

131
00:08:49,934 --> 00:08:50,532
variable.

132
00:08:50,982 --> 00:08:55,804
You can also join Strings with the plus
sign. That's called concatenation.

133
00:08:56,954 --> 00:09:02,706
Your turn. String s equals sunshine. What
is s dot substring of three and six? And

134
00:09:02,706 --> 00:09:04,740
what is s dot index Of shine?

135
00:09:05,190 --> 00:09:06,470
Pause the video now.

136
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:13,163
Indexes three to five are s, h, i. So the
substring is s h i. And shine starts at

137
00:09:13,163 --> 00:09:14,088
index three.

138
00:09:14,538 --> 00:09:19,829
That's the end of Unit one. Next, we'll
start Unit two: selection and iteration.

139
00:09:20,979 --> 00:09:25,656
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

140
00:09:25,656 --> 00:09:26,931
miss the next lesson.

141
00:09:27,381 --> 00:09:28,277
See you there.

